;;; A disjunction nested inside a conjunction, in a rule only a commitment
;;; activates: four solve questions answer and five stop — M1f **W62**,
;;; register row `W62/nested-or-under-commitment`, filed by S1f.5.21
;;; (T1f.5.21.1, 2026-11-10).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `r` has a parameter, so it is compiled once per activator `(r …)`, and
;;; only `mk` writes one, from an `(s …)`, which no fact holds and the
;;; candidate `(s k)` brings. Root compiles no plan of `r`; a commitment
;;; holding `(s k)` does.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An `(or …)` is a whole `:match`, so `r` is refused at load, whether or
;;; not a state activates it: every command exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `864b16ed`, release, the commands disagreed: `ein saturate`, `ein
;;; solve --minimal`, `--single` and `-n 1` answered, exit 0, never compiling
;;; `r`; `ein solve` (describe), `--maximal`, `--count`, `oracle` and `render
;;; lattice` stopped at the commitment that compiles it, exit 1, on the
;;; compiler's line. Refused at load since S1f.5.21 (T1f.5.21.2).

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation s Thing :why "{?1} is an s")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?1} is a q")
(is-a k Thing)
(a k)
(rule mk () :match (s ?x) :assert (r a) :why "{?x} is an s, so r is activated over a")
(rule r (?R)
  :match  (and (?R ?x) (or (p ?x) (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x)
  :why "{?R} holds of {?x}, and p or q does, so p does")
